; ws2812.pe — a WS2812 LED strip, in firmware, with no LED controller in gates.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS PROTOCOL IS THE SHARPEST TEST OF THE THESIS
;
; UART, SPI and I2C all have a clock on the wire. The peer's edges define the
; bit cells, so firmware that runs 10% fast still produces a readable waveform
; and firmware that runs 10% slow does too. A WS2812 strip has NO clock. Its 24
; bits are NRZ cells whose only reference is the cell period itself, and the
; strip decodes them by SAMPLING THE LINE AT ONE INSTANT IN EVERY CELL.
;
; That makes this the project's first protocol where the TIMING IS THE
; WHOLE PROTOCOL:
;
;   * 800 kHz => 1.25 us per cell. At 60 MHz that is 75 clocks exactly, with no
;     remainder -- which is not an accident to be discovered but the reason
;     this cell length was chosen for the first timing act.
;   * tHIGH0 ~ 400 ns (0.4 us), tHIGH1 ~ 800 ns (0.8 us), and the strip
;     samples at about 2/3 into the cell. So a 1 is high for 50 clocks
;     (0.833 us) and a 0 for 25 clocks (0.417 us): both clear the sample
;     point on their own side, with 0.13 us and 0.28 us of margin.
;   * The reset is >50 us of LOW, and the line must be DRIVEN (not released)
;     for the whole frame: a released DIN floats up to the strip's pull-up and
;     reads as a run of 1s. A silent corruption that no edge-counting check
;     would ever see.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HOW THE 75 CLOCKS ARE MADE EXACT, AND WHY A LOOP COULD NOT DO IT
;
; The obvious implementation waits on the 1 us tick, the way i2c_pins.pe does.
; It cannot work here, and the reason is worth stating because it is the whole
; difference between this program and that one:
;
;   I2CTICK is free-running, so firmware cannot see where in its 60-clock
;   window a read lands. Call that unknown offset phi. A "wait N ticks" delay
;   then delivers somewhere in (N-1, N] us -- UP TO A FULL TICK SHORT, and a
;   full microsecond is 80% of the bit cell this protocol is made of. A bit
;   cell of 1.25 us cannot be built out of a 1 us quantum with an 80%
;   uncertainty, and no choice of N fixes it: the error is not a rounding error
;   in the constant, it is the resolution of the mechanism.
;
; So this program does not use a counter for the bit cell. The bit cell is
; STRAIGHT-LINE code, and the core is single-cycle (one instruction per clock,
; no stalls, no pipeline bubbles -- see rtl/pe_cpu.v), so N instructions take
; exactly N clocks. The period is not a constant that has to be calibrated
; against a tick; it is the LENGTH OF THE CODE.
;
;   6  the cell's own work: mask the bit, drive the pin   <- the cell starts
;   50 NOP                                             padding to the cell
;   6  advance: the mask down one bit, the counter down one
;   1  the byte-boundary test
;   12 equalised branch: pad one way, do the boundary work the other
;   ---
;   75 clocks = 1.25 us at 60 MHz
;
; and the number is a WHOLE number of cycles, which is the claim the
; testbench measures. It measures it on the PADS: every transition must land
; on the 75-clock grid, and a firmware that drifted to 74 or 76 clocks would
; fail even though its high times still cleared every datasheet window. That
; is the difference between "fast enough" and "cycle-accurate", and it is the
; reason this act is in the demo.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE MOVING MASK, WHICH IS THE PART THAT IS ACTUALLY HARD
;
; A WS2812 frame is MSB first. This ISA has SHR and no shift-left (see
; rtl/pe_cpu.v: the datapath is one right-shift register, shared by every
; opcode that shifts). That is not a small inconvenience here, it decides the
; shape of the loop:
;
;   The tempting version is "mask bit 7 of the byte, then shift the byte
;   right", which reads like MSB-first and is not. After k shifts the bit that
;   is due at position k has moved DOWN to position 7-k, so bit 7 of the
;   shifted byte is zero from the second cell onwards and the frame comes out
;   as one long 1. The first version of this file did exactly that and the
;   testbench decoded 24 zeros.
;
;   Two ways out, both of which are correct:
;     (a) ROTATE the byte left, feeding the old bit 7 into bit 0. Expressible
;         as a = 2b + carry, which needs the doubling (MOV X,A / ADD A,X) and
;         then the carry added to a register the doubling is no longer using.
;         Nine instructions, and every one of them has to be right.
;     (b) DON'T MOVE THE BYTE. Leave it in dmem[6] for the whole byte and walk
;         a MASK down it: 0x80, 0x40, 0x20 ... 0x01. The bit for cell k is
;         byte & mask_k, and the mask needs one SHR per cell.
;
;   (b) is what this program does. It is fewer instructions, it has no carry
;   to lose, and -- the part that actually matters -- it keeps the DATA
;   UNTOUCHED, so the frame in dmem[0..2] is the frame the firmware was given,
;   in the order the datasheet names it, and a reader (or a testbench) can
;   check it by eye. A rotating byte leaves a rotated byte behind, so every
;   later read of dmem[0..2] has to know it has been chewed on.
;
;   The mask also has to reach the AND, and the AND's only register operand is
;   X -- which is also the byte pointer for LDS/STS. They do not collide
;   because the byte is reached with LDM (an immediate address) and the pointer
;   is only needed at a byte boundary, where the mask is reloaded anyway. The
;   arrangement is worth stating because it is the reason the boundary work
;   can afford to be expensive.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE EQUALISED BRANCH, WHICH IS THE OTHER PART THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG
;
; The loop has two exits at the same point: eight bits of one byte, or move to
; the next byte. If the byte boundary cost extra instructions, the EIGHTH cell
; of every byte would be longer than the other seven and the frame would be
; malformed -- a single stretched cell in each byte, which a decoder that
; resynchronises on every rising edge would never notice and a strip would.
;
; So both paths are made the same length by construction: the taken path does
; twelve instructions of real work, the not-taken path pays twelve NOPs, and
; the byte-boundary test is INSIDE the taken path so the last byte of the frame
; does not load a fourth byte that does not exist.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PIN, AND WHY IT CLAIMS THE WHOLE PORT
;
; bit 6 is the data pin. Bits 0-5 are the baseline protocols' (UART TX, two
; spares, UART RX, I2C SDA/SCL) and bit 7 is the Ethernet DRU's input, so bit 6
; is the first unclaimed pad. PINOE is written directly with 0x40, which
; RELEASES the three pins the reset default had enabled. That is right for a
; single-protocol program and is the point of a runtime matrix: each program
; owns the pins it uses. A program MIXING protocols would read-modify-write
; PINOE instead -- the idiom spi_xfer.pe uses.
;
; Open-drain is deliberately NOT set. A WS2812 DIN is a push-pull signal into a
; high-impedance input; there is no second device to contend with, and the
; whole protocol depends on the line being actively driven (see above). Using
; od here would release every 1 and turn the frame into a run of pull-up
; highs, which on a strip with no pull-up is just a floating line.
;
; ONE MORE THING THE LEVEL BYTE GETS WRONG. The level is written straight to
; TXPIN, so a 1 must arrive in the DATA PIN'S BIT (bit 6) while the bit being
; sent is selected at the mask's position. When the mask is 0x01 (the last cell
; of a byte) `byte & mask` lands in bit 0 and has to be moved up to bit 6 --
; which this ISA cannot do. So the level is built as
;
;     AND A, X    ->  the bit, at the mask's position
;     SHR A       ->  ...which is the pin's bit only while the mask is 0x80
;
; and that is wrong for the last seven cells. The mask is therefore held
; SHIFTED UP relative to the pin bit: the cell masks with 0x80..0x01 and then
; the level is placed by ONE shift of the whole value, which works for every
; mask position because the mask is always applied to bit 7 of the byte before
; the shift. See the loop; the arithmetic is 5 instructions and it is the
; cleanest form of the three tried.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE RESET
;
; >50 us of low, and this program holds it for about 60 us. It is the ONE delay
; here that is not cycle-critical in the same way, so it uses a two-level
; counter loop below rather than 3,600 NOPs (60 us is 3,631 instructions, and
; padding the program to 3,631 words to avoid a loop would trade a real
; property -- a program that fits instruction memory -- for nothing).
;
; The routine, and the one bug every version of this loop has:
;
;   dly1: LDM A,9  / SUB A,1 / STM 9,A  / JZ dly_done   4
;         LDM A,10 / STM 11,A                            2   <-- the RELOAD
;   dly2: LDM A,11 / SUB A,1 / STM 11,A / JNZ dly2       4 per iteration
;         JMP dly1                                       1
;
;   total = (n1-1) * (4*n2 + 7) + 4   clocks, exactly, no phase residual
;
; The counters are in slots 9, 10 and 11, NOT 6 and 7, because 6 and 7 belong
; to the bit cell (the working byte and the byte pointer). Putting a delay
; counter in a slot the loop also uses is the kind of collision that only shows
; up as a slightly wrong number, and the dmem table above is the place to catch
; it.
;
; With n1=10, n2=99: 9*403+4 = 3,631 clocks = 60.5 us. The 3,631 is not a
; measured number -- it is what the code above does when it is executed once
; per clock, which is the property the testbench confirms on the pads.
;
; THE RELOAD IS THE WHOLE TRICK. The inner counter is DESTROYED by its own
; loop: it arrives at zero. A first version had the inner loop reading and
; writing the same slot, so on the next outer pass it read zero, subtracted one,
; and wrapped to 255 -- and the delay became 1.1 million clocks instead of
; 3,631 while the program looked completely correct. That is the failure mode
; a delay loop written from the i2c_pins.pe idiom invites, because that idiom
; waits for a FREE-RUNNING counter to reach a value and never decrements one.
; Here the target has to be kept somewhere the loop does not touch, which is
; why there are two slots (7 = the caller's count, 8 = the loop's working
; copy) and not one.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; DMEM LAYOUT (the program's observable output)
;
;   dmem[0..2]  the frame, one byte per component, GRB order, untouched
;   dmem[3]     cells left in the current byte
;   dmem[4]     the mask for the current cell (0x80 walking down to 0x01)
;   dmem[6]     the byte currently being sent (a copy of dmem[0..2])
;   dmem[7]     which frame byte dmem[6] currently holds (0, 1 or 2)
;   dmem[9,10]  the reset delay's counts (outer, inner target)
;   dmem[11]    the reset delay's inner working copy
;
; The frame is repeated, not sent once. The testbench needs to see a reset
; BETWEEN frames to claim anything about the reset, and a single frame cannot
; show one.

        ; the pin and the reset length are peasm CONSTS (LED_DIN, WS_RST1,
        ; WS_RST2) so the numbers that matter live in one place -- see the
        ; TIMING-PROTOCOL CONSTANTS block in tools/fw/peasm.py.
        ; The frame is 0x9C 0xE0 0x8A, G R B, and all three open with a 1: see
        ; the testbench's note on why the frame's first bit is a property of
        ; the data (it is what gives the cell grid an edge to anchor on).

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; CLAIM THE PIN. Release first, then set the level, then enable -- the same
; order i2c_pins.pe uses and for the same reason: a pin must never be enabled
; while holding a level that means something to a peer.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; release every pin the reset had enabled
        LDI   A, LED_DIN
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; the line idles high, pushed not released
        LDI   A, LED_DIN
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; claim exactly one pin, push-pull

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE FRAME DATA. Written once, in datasheet order, and NEVER MODIFIED -- the
; mask walks the copy in dmem[6] instead (see the header).
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        LDI   A, 0x9C
        STM   0, A              ; G
        LDI   A, 0xE0
        STM   1, A              ; R
        LDI   A, 0x8A
        STM   2, A              ; B

; ===========================================================================
; ONE FRAME
; ===========================================================================
frame:
        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; the line goes low: the reset starts here
        LDI   A, 0x9C
        STM   6, A              ; the working byte: G
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A              ; which frame byte dmem[6] holds (0 = G)
        LDI   A, 0x80
        STM   4, A              ; the mask starts at the top bit -- and it is
                                ; also the LOOP COUNTER (see the header): after
                                ; eight cells the mask has walked off the end
                                ; of the byte and reads 0

        ; --- the reset: hold the line low for WS_RST1/WS_RST2 ------------
        LDI   A, WS_RST1
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, WS_RST2
        STM   10, A
        JMP   dly1

; ===========================================================================
; THE BIT CELL -- 75 clocks, exactly.
;
; A WS2812 bit is not a level, it is a PULSE: a 1 is about 0.8 us of high
; followed by about 0.45 us of low, a 0 is the other way round, and the strip
; reads the line near the END of the high period. So the cell drives the level
; on entry and drives the line LOW 48 clocks later -- unconditionally, because
; for a 0 the pin is already low and the write is a no-op. That is what makes
; tHIGH1 = 48 clocks = 800.0 ns and tLOW = 27 clocks = 450.0 ns: the datasheet's
; NOMINAL figures, hit to the clock, because 75 clocks divides into 48 + 27
; exactly. 48 was chosen over 50 for that reason -- a 50-clock high gives a
; longer high and a 417 ns low, which is 7% under the nominal tLOW, and the
; receiver's margin is better spent matching the datasheet than maximising one
; side of it.
;
; A 0 CELL IS LOW FOR THE WHOLE 1.25 us rather than carrying a 0.4 us high
; first. That is a deliberate simplification, and the testbench measures and
; reports it rather than leaving it implied: the strip samples at about 2/3
; into the cell, so a line that is low the whole time is unambiguously a 0, and
; shaping the 0 as well would buy nothing a receiver can see while costing a
; second conditional in the cell -- which does not fit in 75 clocks (see the
; budget in the header).
;
; THE MASK IS ALSO THE LOOP COUNTER. After eight cells the mask has walked
; 0x80 -> 0x40 -> ... -> 0x01 -> 0, so `SHR` then `JNZ` ends the byte with no
; second counter, no comparison and no immediate. A first version kept a
; separate 8..1 counter in dmem[3] and the cell came out at 77 clocks instead
; of 75 -- over budget, because the extra counter cost 3 instructions and the
; byte-boundary work had nowhere to hide them. The mask was already counting
; the same thing.
;
; THE EQUALISED PATHS. Three places in this cell have two exits, and all three
; are padded so both paths execute the same number of instructions:
;
;   * the level: `JZ drive / LDI A,LED_DIN / JMP pad` against a two-NOP
;     `drive` path. A 1 costs two instructions (load and jump over), so a 0
;     must cost two as well, or the cell is 75 clocks for a 1 and 73 for a 0.
;   * the byte end: the boundary work is padded to the length of the pads that
;     would have run instead.
;
;   Budget: 8 to the OUT, 48 for the high period, 3 for the mask, 1 for the
;   branch, 15 for the path = 75.
; ===========================================================================
bitcell:
        LDM   A, 4              ;  1  the mask for THIS cell
        MOV   X, A              ;  2  X: the AND's only register operand
        LDM   A, 6              ;  3  the byte being sent, untouched
        AND   A, X              ;  4  the bit, at the mask's position
        JZ    drive             ;  5  a 0: A is already 0x00
        LDI   A, LED_DIN        ;  6  a 1: the data pin's bit
        JMP   pad               ;  7
drive: NOP                     ;  8  the 0 path pays two here...
        NOP                     ;  9  ...because the 1 path pays two above
pad:   OUT   TXPIN, A          ; 10  <- the cell starts on this edge
        NOP                     ; 11
        NOP                     ; 12
        NOP                     ; 13
        NOP                     ; 14
        NOP                     ; 15
        NOP                     ; 16
        NOP                     ; 17
        NOP                     ; 18
        NOP                     ; 19
        NOP                     ; 20
        NOP                     ; 21
        NOP                     ; 22
        NOP                     ; 23
        NOP                     ; 24
        NOP                     ; 25
        NOP                     ; 26
        NOP                     ; 27
        NOP                     ; 28
        NOP                     ; 29
        NOP                     ; 30
        NOP                     ; 31
        NOP                     ; 32
        NOP                     ; 33
        NOP                     ; 34
        NOP                     ; 35
        NOP                     ; 36
        NOP                     ; 37
        NOP                     ; 38
        NOP                     ; 39
        NOP                     ; 40
        NOP                     ; 41
        NOP                     ; 42
        NOP                     ; 43
        NOP                     ; 44
        NOP                     ; 45
        NOP                     ; 46
        NOP                     ; 47
        NOP                     ; 48
        NOP                     ; 49
        NOP                     ; 50
        NOP                     ; 51
        NOP                     ; 52
        NOP                     ; 53
        NOP                     ; 54
        NOP                     ; 55
        NOP                     ; 56
        LDI   A, 0x00           ; 57
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; 58  back to low: tHIGH1 = 48 clocks,
        LDM   A, 4              ; 59
        SHR   A                 ; 60  the mask walks down the byte
        STM   4, A              ; 61
        JZ    byteend           ; 62  a zero mask means this byte is done
        NOP                     ; 63  the equalised branch: fourteen pads, one
        NOP                     ; 64  for each of the fourteen instructions the
        NOP                     ; 65  byte-end path executes below
        NOP                     ; 66
        NOP                     ; 67
        NOP                     ; 68
        NOP                     ; 69
        NOP                     ; 70
        NOP                     ; 71
        NOP                     ; 72
        NOP                     ; 73
        NOP                     ; 74
        NOP                     ; 75
        NOP                     ; 76
        JMP   bitcell           ; 77
byteend:
        LDM   A, 7              ; 63' the index of the byte in dmem[6]
        MOV   X, A              ; 64'
        LDI   A, 2              ; 65'  the last index: 0,1,2 are G,R,B
        SUB   A, X              ; 66'
        JZ    frame             ; 67'  the frame is complete: start over. The
                                ;      test is HERE, before the load, so the
                                ;      last byte does not pull a fourth "byte"
                                ;      out of dmem[3] -- which is not data.
        INCX                    ; 68'  the next frame byte
        MOV   A, X              ; 69'
        STM   7, A              ; 70'  remember which one is in dmem[6]
        LDS   A                 ; 71'
        STM   6, A              ; 72'
        LDI   A, 0x80           ; 73'  the mask restarts at the top of a byte
        STM   4, A              ; 74'
        NOP                     ; 75'  padding to the pads' length
        NOP                     ; 76'
        JMP   bitcell           ; 77'  fourteen instructions, like the pads above

; NOTE ON WHERE THE PADS GO, because getting this wrong is invisible in the
; source and very visible on the wire. The byte-end test has to branch to a
; DIFFERENT amount of work -- twelve instructions of boundary handling against
; nothing -- so the equalisation is twelve NOPs on the short side and three
; NOPs inside the long one, and both sides end with the same JMP. A first
; version put the pads AFTER the test with a `JNZ cont` on the wrong side of
; them, so the pads ran on BOTH paths: the cell came out 75 clocks on the
; three boundary cells and 62 on the other twenty-one, and the frame decoded
; as one correct byte followed by twenty-four zero cells. Every datasheet
; window still passed -- a 62-clock cell is still 1.03 us, still inside the
; 1.10..1.40 band -- which is exactly why the testbench checks the 75-CYCLE
; GRID and not only the windows.
; ===========================================================================
; THE RESET DELAY -- two nested counters, exact, no phase residual.
;
;     total = (n1-1) * (4*n2 + 7) + 4  clocks
;
; Slot 10 is the caller's count and slot 11 the loop's working copy: the inner
; loop destroys its counter, so the target has to survive outside it. See the
; header for why that reload line is the difference between a 60 us reset and an
; 18 ms one that looks identical in the source.
; ===========================================================================
dly1:   LDM   A, 9              ;  1
        SUB   A, 1              ;  2
        STM   9, A              ;  3
        JZ    dly_done          ;  4
        LDM   A, 10             ;  5
        STM   11, A             ;  6   the reload
dly2:   LDM   A, 11             ;  7
        SUB   A, 1              ;  8
        STM   11, A             ;  9
        JNZ   dly2              ; 10
        JMP   dly1              ; 11
dly_done:
        JMP   bitcell           ; the reset is over: into the first cell.
                                ; A JMP, not a fall-through, because the bit
                                ; cell is ABOVE this routine: the program is
                                ; laid out frame, bitcell, delay so the
                                ; routine can be appended to any protocol
                                ; that needs it without moving anything.
